good evening ladies and gentlemen i'd  just like to  open this evening with a tribute to  sir david amis and i will introduce you  to  reverend richard iles who will be  leading the service thank you  good evening ladies and gentlemen  we gathered here this evening at this  time  to  remember  the tragic death  of sir david amos  and a moment of prayer  before we lead  a moment of silence  for god in the busyness and  the chaotic nature of our lives  we come this moment  at this time  to remember before god  those that have been lost  in tragic circumstances  and as we gather to do business later  may we spend a few moments in  remembering  sir david amos  and all the good that he did for our  community  in this area  and  nationally  and we ask these things in the name of  jesus christ  our savior  amen amen  i think it would be appropriate if we  stand  three minutes  please be seated  we're now going to listen  to a number  of tributes  and i'm going to call upon  chairman of the council councillor mrs  julie good  it is with great sadness  that we pay tribute to the memory of sir  david amis  david was one of the kindest and  compassionate politicians  who touched the lives  of so many  during his extensive public service  he had the ability to listen to people  and make them feel valued  he wasn't afraid to challenge what he  believed in to make a difference  on occasion  david lent me his personal support as he  in common with me  was passionate about supporting our nhs  i recall one occasion when i had sailed  down the hospital tower at south end  hospital to raise funds  for the renal unit  he went out of his way to take the time  to listen to why this was so important  to me  more recently  david celebrated the nhs birthday at an  event held at saxon hall earlier this  year  followed  an exceedingly challenging period for  everyone the fact that he commemorated  the nhs event in the company of local  constituents  was greatly appreciated  the outpouring of love  in the aftermath of the tragic event  resulting in the loss of sir david  is testament to the wonderful man he was  and what he meant to our local community  as a council i hope these overwhelming  tributes may bring small comfort to sir  david's family and my sincere heartfelt  condolences go to david's family and  loved ones  at this most devastating time of their  lives god bless them all  i'm now going to call  on  the leader of the council councillor  simon wooten  who will pay tribute to sir david banks  thank you  [Music]  no decent person could help but to feel  appalled and shocked at the violent and  tragic manner in which sir david amis  was murdered on friday the 15th of  october  since then  we have heard so many tributes and kind  words said about him  a huge number of people will have known  sir david from a variety of perspectives  i had met him on many occasions  the latest at a dinner just a few weeks  before he died where he was the guest  speaker  we sat near each other  i was asked and it was my privilege to  give the vote of thanks following his  excellent speech  indeed i was due to be at another dinner  with him on the evening of the day he  died  what can i add that hasn't already been  said in the very kind tributes to sir  david over these past few weeks  probably nothing  he was a kind and caring man  who saw his duty  as an mp to work hard for his residents  and to do the best for the community he  represented  so well in order to make it a better  place in which to live  he was engaging and passionate about  things he believed in  he had a good sense of humor  he was deeply religious  he loved and cared about his family who  our thoughts and sympathies are  particularly with at this sad and  difficult time  he was i believe a role mod model to us  all  and was an example about how to live  life to the full  so david you will be hugely missed in so  many ways and from a variety of  perspectives  rest in peace  thank you chairman  i now call  upon the deputy leader of the council  councillor sheryl rowe to pay a tribute  to sedate davis  thank you  sir david was a great friend to many of  us  our thoughts and prayers are with sir  david's family as we mourn for a man who  gave his life to the people of south end  so david's legacy of creating a city is  to be remembered as a brilliant  achievement  and his devote devotion to his community  over 40 years is a testament to his  strength of character and caring nature  he will be missed dearly and we pray he  rests in peace  would any other members like to make a  tribute  thank you chairman  i've known david amos for as long as  i've been a  district councillor for 17 years  and there's two  things i remember him most on  once when i was chairman of this council  he invited me to a  centenary um  function  where his mother was supposed to be  invited  and it was all for the elderly residents  at his particular home  we had a lovely a lovely party  and i spoke to one of the residents  there and the president said to me  we don't have any tea dances now do we  and i said well we can do  and i asked him to dance  and david amos came up to me afterwards  and said to me  i knew it'd be you i knew it'd be you to  get up and ask the elderly residents to  dance  that was the first thing i always  remember with david the second one  i was down at b and q one day with my  late partner  and who was walking up and down the  aisles was david amos hello he said what  are you doing down here i said oh we're  just pottering about what you don't down  here  well i'm having to take photographs of  lights because my wife wants a new light  in the house  and we stood there and laughed and joked  about certain things and there's two  memorable things i will always remember  about david  thank you chairman  um  thank you  i first met sir david  when my mother's friend introduced us in  the late 80s  when he was mp for basildon  we had much in common  born in the east end  with clara and blue running through our  veins  and a love of pugs  clara and blue is west ham for those who  don't know  he was a committed politician  working hard for basildon  and showed passion and compassion  for all  he was a man of the people  people of all political colors respected  him  always working  answered all letters and phone calls  he was a dedicated to his work  and a true gentleman  i would see david occasionally at that  time  and he always remembered my name  then in 1997  he became mp for southend  our children then went to the same  school  and became friends  as young children do they had sleepovers  and stayed at each other's houses  so i got to know him more  as a family man and a friend  friday's sleepovers were better for them  as on a sunday his girls would often  have to get up to go to church  and after a night of not sleeping that  was not very good  they always had fun  on one occasion  when staying with david and julia  david got locked in the study overnight  we never found out who was the  ringleader  but he took it in goodsport  as he always did  locking up a night of the realm  david was a wonderful speaker  both formerly in the house of commons  and informally at private private  dinners and events  he was eloquent  clear and persuasive  with his passion on subjects  showing through  informally he was amusing  interesting  and sometimes a little cheeky  leaving  us all  wanting to hear more  the last occasion i saw him  was just after his daughter got married  and just before my youngest daughter's  wedding  this september  we discuss weddings speeches  and where have the years gone  but how happy we were  that our children were happy and settled  david loved his family  he loved his city  he loved animals  and he was a true patriot  he worked hard for each and every one  valuing all equally  he had many causes  one was animal welfare  speaking about tethering horses  and mules  and crating hens and cruelty to pigs  and he always cared about dogs  he loved  all living creatures  his last words to me were  see you soon  i wish  god bless david  rest in peace  condolences to julia and the family  thank you  now we'll have councillor steptoe  i first met david uh when i was chairman  of the carnival association in south end  and it was my task to get both mps and  the mayor of south end  doing things that are well out of their  comfort zone  especially for the carnival  and on one occasion it was the  carnival's 100th anniversary  and we had a considerable number of the  ex-carnival queens came  along we hired a  single decker  south end transport bus from the canbu  candy bus museum  and  for the carnival queens to ride along on  the seafront in the carnival  now david came up to me and said what  job have you got for me david  and i said it's great job david  you're in charge of all the old queens  you never quite forgave me for saying  that  but on another occasion  um he asked me to uh do some canvassing  with him  in one of my old cars  little morris eight and it's eight  because it's an eight horsepower  modern car is a couple of hundred  and  i arrived at south end airport to pick  up his passenger  and he was ann whitaker  so there's myself  david  and whitaker and two of his kids  in my little aunt morris eight  and as we're driving around lee i  suddenly realized oh we're heading for  lee hill  and i commented well we might just have  to get out and push  david was chuckling away next to me but  i did get these strange comments coming  from behind me from young anne  and some years later i bumped into anne  and another do  and she really did remember the occasion  that she nearly had to get out and push  tomorrow's 8 with david  david you will be missed  it was great fun and always  good for life  god bless you  councillor  smith  thank you  um  like many others i met david at a  variety of occasions  uh political and social  and when i when i see that picture and  and i've seen the pictures of it  recently  it's it's difficult to imagine what has  happened you just imagine him to become  bounding through the door with that  happy cheerful voice  that he he always had  and i i i very much liked the the  comment that uh  was made shortly after the terrible  event  where he was described while he was  still alive commonly known as the late  david amis as i think we all know his  timekeeping was uh  something to be desired to say to say  the least  but over the years  seeing him as  a a politician in action and seeing the  craft that he could display  of talking to everyone remembering  everyone making everyone feel special he  felt quite privileged to to be in his  presence  it was amazing how he managed to get to  so many places to support so many things  to see so many people  he he  he's gone too soon but by goodness  didn't he do a good job while he was  here  thank you  reverend  there are so many ways in which we can  remember  sir david  very personal  very humorous  filled with intellect  he was a man of incredible integrity  he would talk to anyone  of any faith  or no faith  he was interested  and he would sit or stand and listen and  we have heard so many different ways  i met david on one occasion  where he invited me to go round the  houses of parliament  and as we were wondering he was telling  us all the little snippets and all of  the the interesting fun facts about  the houses of parliament  and he was someone who would make light  but also make serious  and i wonder  if i could  finish by just reminding us  that  we are  left behind  and  we have memories  in so many different ways whether that  is of personal  or political  but the one thing that we need to carry  on for him  is to continue to do what he would love  and see  the city that he has chosen  to be transformed into a a great city  we are not in that city  as councillors  but we are representatives and perhaps  one lesson that we need to learn is to  follow in with in his footsteps by being  people  of integrity  come what may  thank you  thank you  we will now take a short break  and reconvene at 7 30.

